On the twenty-third day of my internship, I continued learning 
about the Linux environment and focused on environment variables 
and configuration files. I reviewed the $PATH, $HOME, $USER, 
and $PWD variables and studied how environment variables 
are used by the shell.

I also read about files such as .bashrc and .profile. 
I learned that these configuration files can contain 
commands and settings that are loaded when a shell 
session starts. I examined the contents of my .bashrc 
file to better understand how the Bash environment 
can be customized.

This helped me connect the concepts I had previously
learned about Bash and environment variables with 
the configuration of an actual Linux user environment.
